---
// about.astro — the honest HOW-IT-WORKS page. A standalone Almanac document
// (no live island, no locality picker): just the method, in plain words. The
// <head> + masthead + nav + footer wrapper come from the shared section shell
// (Page.astro); page-specific styling lives in the scoped <style> below.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";

const title = "How पाऊस works — Mumbai rain nowcast method";
const description =
  "How the free Mumbai rain forecast app works: keyless Open-Meteo, independent METAR labels from VABB airport, calibrated logistic model behind an eval gate. What it claims — and what it doesn't.";
---
<Page title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/about" active="about">
  <!-- Lede -->
  <section class="intro">
    <p class="kicker">Colophon · how it works</p>
    <h1 class="title">An honest machine for <em>one question</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      पाऊस answers a single question — will it rain at your spot in the next
      couple of hours — and tries to answer it well, cheaply, and without
      pretending to be more than it is. Here is the whole machine: the
      architecture, the data, the model, and the line between what it can
      claim and what it can't.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 · architecture -->
  <section class="method">
    <p class="method-kicker"><span class="n">01</span> The architecture</p>
    <h2>Free, even at <em>zero users</em></h2>
    <p>
      There is no server. The page you're reading is plain HTML and CSS; one
      small script runs in <em>your</em> browser, calls the weather API
      directly, and applies a handful of numbers. Nothing runs on a backend
      per visit — no database answering queries, no API key, no credit card.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the work happens in the browser and the data feed is free, the
      running cost is the same at zero users as at a million: nothing. The
      site is served as static files from a CDN; the forecast comes keyless
      from <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>. That's the entire
      bill.
    </p>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>server</dt><dd>none · static CDN</dd></div>
      <div><dt>API key</dt><dd>none required</dd></div>
      <div><dt>compute</dt><dd>your browser</dd></div>
      <div><dt>cost</dt><dd>$0 at any scale</dd></div>
      <div><dt>forecast fetch</dt><dd>keyless Open-Meteo</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 · data -->
  <section class="method">
    <p class="method-kicker"><span class="n">02</span> The data</p>
    <h2>Two streams, kept <em>deliberately apart</em></h2>
    <p>
      The <b>input</b> is forecast. From Open-Meteo we read two models:
      <code>best_match</code> — its tuned multi-model blend, our primary feed —
      and <code>ecmwf_ifs025</code>, ECMWF's flagship operational physics
      model, Europe's gold standard, kept as the benchmark we measure
      ourselves against. Alongside them rides the single strongest 0–2 hour
      clue: how much rain actually fell in the last three hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <b>answer key</b> is not forecast. It's <code>METAR</code> — the real
      sky reports filed at Mumbai airport (<code>VABB</code>, Chhatrapati
      Shivaji) by trained observers and instruments, free and keyless. We read
      the present-weather code — <code>RA</code> and <code>DZ</code> mean rain;
      mist, haze and fog don't — into a plain yes-or-no: did it rain this hour?
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      This separation is the whole point. We never grade a forecast against
      another forecast — that would make "we're accurate" a tautology. The
      label is an independent ground observation, so the correction we learn
      from it is honest rather than circular.
    </p>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>forecast</dt><dd>Open-Meteo best_match</dd></div>
      <div><dt>benchmark</dt><dd>ECMWF-IFS · ecmwf_ifs025</dd></div>
      <div><dt>top feature</dt><dd>rain, last 3&nbsp;h</dd></div>
      <div><dt>ground truth</dt><dd>METAR · VABB airport</dd></div>
      <div><dt>rain tokens</dt><dd>RA · DZ</dd></div>
      <div><dt>forecast vs label</dt><dd>independent</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 · model -->
  <section class="method">
    <p class="method-kicker"><span class="n">03</span> The model</p>
    <h2>A few numbers, applied in <em>your browser</em></h2>
    <p>
      The model is a calibrated logistic classifier. Five inputs — the two
      forecasts, the hour of day (as sine and cosine), and the recent rain —
      go in; one corrected probability of rain comes out. That's all of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's trained offline and exported to <code>model.json</code>: a few
      kilobytes of weights and an intercept. The browser applies them with one
      line of arithmetic — <code>sigmoid(w·x + b)</code>. No machine-learning
      engine runs on the live path, no GPU, no inference server. The "AI" is
      already reduced to multiplication and addition before it ever reaches you.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it sharpens itself, for free. An hourly job on GitHub Actions logs
      each forecast next to what METAR later observed — a growing diary
      (<code>log.csv</code>, versioned in Git). A daily job retrains on that
      diary. A fresh model only replaces the live one if it beats both the
      current champion <em>and</em> the raw forecast on a time-ordered holdout —
      a walk-forward test with no peeking at the future. So the served model
      can only improve or hold; it can't quietly regress.
    </p>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>model</dt><dd>logistic · 5 features</dd></div>
      <div><dt>served as</dt><dd>sigmoid(w·x + b)</dd></div>
      <div><dt>file</dt><dd>model.json · few&nbsp;KB</dd></div>
      <div><dt>logging</dt><dd>hourly · Actions cron</dd></div>
      <div><dt>retrain</dt><dd>daily · eval-gated</dd></div>
      <div><dt>promotion</dt><dd>beats champion + raw</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 · claims -->
  <section class="method">
    <p class="method-kicker"><span class="n">04</span> The claims</p>
    <h2>What it does — and <em>doesn't</em> — claim</h2>
    <p>
      Honesty here means stating the bar, not boasting a number. The eval gate
      refuses to ship a model that doesn't clear the obvious baselines on a
      fair, time-ordered holdout — and is equally clear about the league it
      isn't playing in.
    </p>
    <div class="claims">
      <div class="claim is-does">
        <h3>What it's built to beat</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Persistence</b> — "same as the last hour."</li>
          <li><b>Climatology</b> — the seasonal base rate.</li>
          <li>The <b>raw forecast</b>, including raw ECMWF-IFS, on its own rain call.</li>
          <li>On the measures that matter for a yes/no call: <b>calibration</b> (Brier score) and <b>false-alarm rate</b> — the forecast's wrong rain warnings.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="claim is-doesnt">
        <h3>What it doesn't claim</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>To out-predict <b>frontier AI</b> weather models (GraphCast, AIFS and the like) at their own game.</li>
          <li>To use <b>live radar</b> — there's none on the free path. This is a calibrated correction of a public forecast for one city.</li>
          <li>Any number you can't check: the live model's real scorecard is computed on logged outcomes, not asserted here.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- live model colophon — progressive enhancement; meaningful without JS -->
  <section class="colophon" aria-labelledby="colophon-h">
    <p class="method-kicker" id="colophon-h"><span class="n">·</span> The model serving you right now</p>
    <p>
      Everything above is carried by one small file, <code>model.json</code>.
      Its version and last-retrain date are stamped inside it; the full
      scorecard — Brier score against the raw forecast and against climatology,
      on real logged outcomes — lives on the <a href="/scoreboard">Scoreboard</a>.
    </p>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>version</dt><dd id="m-version">—</dd></div>
      <div><dt>last retrained</dt><dd id="m-trained">—</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <footer slot="footer" class="foot">
    <p class="colo">
      Open by design — every step above is a small, inspectable piece. The
      repository is private for now, so there's no public link yet.
    </p>
    <p class="attrib">
      Forecasts: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/" rel="noopener">Open-Meteo</a>.
      Rain truth: METAR via NOAA Aviation Weather (VABB).
    </p>
  </footer>
</Page>

<!-- Tiny, standalone enhancement: stamp the live model's version + retrain
     date from /model.json. No island dependency, no motion. If the fetch
     fails the static "—" / Scoreboard fallback simply stays. -->
<script>
  const elV = document.getElementById("m-version");
  const elT = document.getElementById("m-trained");
  const MONTHS = ["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"];

  function fmtDate(iso) {
    const p = String(iso).slice(0, 10).split("-");
    if (p.length !== 3) return String(iso).slice(0, 10);
    const mi = Number(p[1]) - 1;
    const mon = MONTHS[mi] || p[1];
    return `${Number(p[2])} ${mon} ${p[0]}`;
  }

  (async () => {
    try {
      const r = await fetch("/model.json", { cache: "no-store" });
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`model.json ${r.status}`);
      const m = await r.json();
      if (elV) elV.textContent = m.version != null ? `v${m.version}` : (m.type || "live");
      if (elT && m.trained_at) elT.textContent = fmtDate(m.trained_at);
    } catch (err) {
      // Calm fallback — keep the page whole, point to the live scorecard.
      if (elV) elV.textContent = "see Scoreboard";
      console.error("about: couldn't read model.json", err);
    }
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* The centered column + masthead + section nav come from Page.astro /
     almanac.css. Only this document's own type + spec styling lives here. */

  /* ---- lede ---- */
  .intro { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-4); }
  .kicker {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .62rem;
    letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .title {
    font-family: "Fraunces", Georgia, serif;
    font-weight: 900; font-variation-settings: "opsz" 144;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 8.5vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: .98; letter-spacing: -.02em; text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .title em { font-style: italic; font-weight: 600; }
  .lede {
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif; font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(1.12rem, 4.6vw, 1.4rem); line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink); max-width: 36rem;
  }

  /* ---- method sections ---- */
  .method { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-4); }
  .method-kicker {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .6em;
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .62rem;
    letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .method-kicker .n { color: var(--radar-rain); font-weight: 600; }
  .method h2 {
    font-family: "Fraunces", Georgia, serif;
    font-weight: 900; font-variation-settings: "opsz" 72;
    font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 6vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.03; letter-spacing: -.015em; text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .method h2 em { font-style: italic; font-weight: 600; }
  .method p {
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.02rem, 4vw, 1.12rem); line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink); max-width: 38rem;
  }
  .method p.muted { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .method p b { font-weight: 600; }

  /* inline technical bits */
  code {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .82em;
    background: var(--paper-2); border: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; color: var(--ink);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  a {
    color: var(--radar-rain);
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  a:hover, a:focus-visible { color: var(--ink); }

  /* ---- mono spec ledger (echoes the home detail rows) ---- */
  .spec { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin-top: 2px; }
  .spec > div {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s-4); padding: 10px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .spec dt {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .64rem;
    letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .spec dd {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .82rem;
    color: var(--ink); text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* ---- claims: does / doesn't ---- */
  .claims { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-4); }
  .claim {
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4);
    background: var(--paper-2); border-left: 3px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .claim.is-does   { border-left-color: var(--radar-dry); }
  .claim.is-doesnt { border-left-color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .claim h3 {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .64rem;
    letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .claim.is-does h3 { color: var(--radar-dry); }
  .claim ul { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 9px; }
  .claim li {
    position: relative; padding-left: 1.3em;
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .claim li b { font-weight: 600; }
  .claim li::before {
    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0;
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .8em; line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .claim.is-does li::before   { content: "\25B8"; color: var(--radar-dry); }   /* ▸ */
  .claim.is-doesnt li::before { content: "\2013"; color: var(--ink-soft); }    /* – */
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .claims { flex-direction: row; align-items: stretch; }
    .claim { flex: 1 1 0; }
  }

  /* ---- live-model colophon card ---- */
  .colophon {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-3);
    padding: var(--s-4); background: var(--paper-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .colophon p {
    font-family: "Newsreader", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink); max-width: 38rem;
  }
  .colophon .method-kicker { margin: 0; }

  /* ---- footer (about runs a heavier 2px ink rule than the global .foot) ---- */
  .foot {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;
    padding-top: var(--s-4); border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace; font-size: .7rem;
    letter-spacing: .02em; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .foot .colo { line-height: 1.5; max-width: 40rem; }
</style>
